    "created_at": "2021-12-02T17:03:36Z",
    "updated_at": "2021-12-02T17:03:36Z",
    "body": "> Unfortunately, starship (by design) is essentially completely stateless. Any information that needs to be passed between draws (like commmand duration) is handled by the shell\r\n\r\nThat's not really stateless, that's offloading your state storage/management to the shell hooks instead - and in doing so it becomes restricted read-only state from the executable's side. That does sound rather fragile and it wouldn't be that odd to use `$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/starship/$STARSHIP_SESSION_KEY` or something as a store for transient session state instead, but that's a little out of scope here...\r\n\r\nI'm sure there's a simple solution on the zsh side involving detecting / keeping track of repeated draws, but yeah I'm not familiar enough with zsh to know how to best handle it.",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
